## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
Tom prepares for a series of four equally time-consuming exams. He spends 20 minutes preparing before starting his first exam at 8:00 AM. He completes his second exam at 10:30 AM. When does he finish his fourth exam?
$\textbf{(A)}\; \text{12:30 PM} \qquad\textbf{(B)}\; \text{1:00 PM} \qquad\textbf{(C)}\; \text{1:30 PM} \qquad\textbf{(D)}\; \text{2:00 PM} \qquad\textbf{(E)}\; \text{2:30 PM}$
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
1. **Calculate the total time for the first two exams**: Tom starts his exams at 8:00 AM (after 20 minutes of preparation starting at 7:40 AM) and finishes his second exam at 10:30 AM. The total time taken for the first two exams is:
   \[
   \text{Total time} = 10:30 \text{ AM} - 8:00 \text{ AM} = 2 \text{ hours and 30 minutes} = 150 \text{ minutes}.
   \]

2. **Determine the duration of one exam**: As the exams are equally time-consuming and two exams took a total of 150 minutes, the duration of one exam is:
   \[
   \text{Duration of one exam} = \frac{150 \text{ minutes}}{2} = 75 \text{ minutes}.
   \]

3. **Calculate the finish time of the fourth exam**: The third exam starts immediately after the second exam at 10:30 AM, and the fourth exam starts immediately after the third. Adding twice the duration of one exam (2 x 75 minutes = 150 minutes) to the end time of the second exam gives:
   \[
   \text{Finish time of fourth exam} = 10:30 \text{ AM} + 150 \text{ minutes} = 1:00 \text{ PM}.
   \]

Thus, Tom finishes his fourth exam at $1:00 \text{ PM}$.
The final answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(B)}\; \text{1:00 PM}}$
</GROUND_TRUTH>

