To estimate the range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy, we use the Heisenberg uncertainty principle:
\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \geq \frac{\hbar}{2}
\]
Given the lifetime \(\Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s, the uncertainty in energy is:
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\hbar}{2 \Delta t} = \frac{1.055 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J·s}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 2.64 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
\]
Converting this to MeV (1 eV = \(1.602 \times 10^{-19}\) J):
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{2.64 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J/MeV}} \approx 16.5 \text{ MeV}
\]
Thus, the typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:
\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 16.5 \text{ MeV}
\]
So, the range is \(\boxed{1368.5 \text{ MeV} \text{ to } 1401.5 \text{ MeV}}\).
</GROUND_TRUTH>

